@page "/"

<PageTitle>Memoria Ecommerce DCB</PageTitle>

<h1>A catalogue built on dynamic consistency boundaries</h1>
<p class="lede">
    An example app for Memoria. Products are event sourced with the DCB package and stored in
    Postgres; the list you browse them in is an ordinary table built from those events.
</p>

<div class="prose">
    <p>
        A SKU has to be unique across the whole catalogue, and that is awkward to enforce with
        streams. A stream per product can tell you whether that product exists, but never whether
        another one has already claimed the SKU. One stream for the catalogue can tell you, at the
        cost of putting every product creation in the shop in a single queue.
    </p>
    <p>
        DCB drops the stream and selects events by tag instead. A product is created inside the
        boundary <code>product:{id} OR sku:{code}</code>, so the decision reads exactly the events
        that could contradict it — and two people adding products at the same moment only get in
        each other's way if they picked the same SKU.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>How a product gets created</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li>
        <span class="step-name">Validate</span>
        <span class="step-detail">
            FluentValidation checks the shape of the command — a name, a SKU, a price that is not
            negative. Nothing here needs the database.
        </span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="step-name">Read and decide</span>
        <span class="step-detail">
            The handler reads where the boundary stands, folds its events into a
            <code>Product</code> aggregate, and asks it to create. The aggregate refuses if the SKU
            is taken, which is the one rule that needs the fold.
        </span>
    </li>
    <li>
        <span class="step-name">Append</span>
        <span class="step-detail">
            <code>ProductCreatedEvent</code> is appended on condition that nothing new arrived
            inside the boundary. A notification then fills the row the products list reads.
        </span>
    </li>
</ol>

